      "text": "To determine the difference between the total number of green tiles and the total number of blue tiles in the new figure created by attaching a border of green tiles, we need to analyze the given figure and the process of attaching the border.\n\nFirst, let's summarize the number of blue and green tiles in the original figure:\n- Blue tiles = 13\n- Green tiles = 6\n\nNext, we need to understand how the border of green tiles is attached. The border is created by attaching green tiles along the outer edge of the original figure. The key is to determine how many green tiles are added to each side of the original figure.\n\nBy examining the original figure, we can see that the blue tiles are aligned in a pattern that allows for a natural border to be formed. The green tiles will be added in such a way that they fill the gaps between the blue tiles on each side.\n\nConsider the following:\n- The original figure has a height of 6 (the top hexagon) and a width of 8 (5 + 3 + 2 + 1 + 2 + 5 = 18 is overcounted, but we focus on the outer edge).\n- When we attach a border of green tiles, the total number of tiles on each side will increase by 2 (one on each adjacent side of the original figure).\n\nThus, we add 2 green tiles to each of the 8 outer tiles (which is one for each side of the 4 pairs of adjacent tiles):\n\\[ 8 \\times 2 = 16 \\text{ green tiles} \\]\n\nNow, let's calculate the total number of tiles in the new figure:\n- The original figure has 13 blue tiles and 6 green tiles, which sums to 19 tiles.\n- Adding the 16 green tiles to the border, the total number of green tiles is:\n\\[ 6 + 16 = 22 \\]\n- The total number of blue tiles remains 13.\n\nFinally, we find the difference between the total number of green tiles and the total number of blue tiles in the new figure:\n\\[ 22 - 13 = 9 \\]\n\nThus, the difference is \\(\\boxed{9}\\).",
