      "sample_index": 2,
      "text": "To solve the problem, we need to determine the difference between the total number of green tiles and the total number of blue tiles in the new figure after adding a border of green tiles.\n\nFirst, let's count the original number of blue and green tiles:\n- There are 13 blue tiles.\n- There are 6 green tiles.\n\nNext, we need to determine how the border of green tiles is added. The border adds 12 green tiles (since it consists of a row of blue tiles at the outer edge forming a 4x3 rectangle: 6 + 6 more green tiles on the sides).\n\nThe new figure will have a total of \\(13 + 6 + 12 = 31\\) tiles of both colors. To find the difference between the total number of green tiles and the total number of blue tiles, we calculate:\n\\[ 12 - 13 = -1 \\]\n\nThus, the difference between the total number of green tiles and the total number of blue tiles in the new figure is \\(\\boxed{-1}\\).\n\nThere is a tradition in the Asibrary to award a special badge to any student who collects exactly 10 books in a single session, using the collection counters as below:\n\n- Each student starts with one badge.\n- On a single day, a student can add one badge or two badges to one of their counters.\n- The only rule is that no two counters can have more than 10 badges combined at any time.\n\nMaria, a student who has added 3 badges to her two counters one time during her session, was wondering, given the intention that no two counters can have more than 10 badges combined, if it is possible for her to end this session with a maximum number of badges when collecting consistently over multiple sessions.\n\nLet's denote the number of badges in Maria's two counters as \\( a \\) and \\( b \\), where \\( a \\geq b \\). Initially, Maria has one badge in each counter, so \\( a = 1 \\) and \\( b = 1 \\).\n\nOn one day, Maria adds 3 badges to one of the counters. There are two possible scenarios:\n\n1. Maria adds 3 badges to \\( a \\). Then \\( a = 4 \\) and \\( b = 1 \\).\n2. Maria adds 3 badges to \\( b \\). Then \\( a = 1 \\) and \\( b = 4 \\).\n\nIn both scenarios, the combined number of badges in the two counters does not exceed 10:\n\n- If Maria adds 3 badges to \\( a \\), then \\( a = 4 \\) and \\( b = 1 \\), so \\( a + b = 4 + 1 = 5 \\).\n- If Maria adds 3 badges to \\( b \\), then \\( a = 1 \\) and \\( b = 4 \\), so \\( a + b = 1 + 4 = 5 \\).\n\nSince in both scenarios, the total number of badges in either counter is less than or equal to 10, it is always possible for Maria to end the session with a maximum number of badges when collecting consistently over multiple sessions.\n\nTherefore, the answer is \\(\\boxed{\\text{Yes}}\\).\n\nElliot drew a right-angle triangle on his book. It had a base of 4 inches, a height of 3 inches, and a certain length of the hypotenuse. What was the length of the perimeter of the triangle that Elliot drew?\n\nTo determine the length of the perimeter of the right-angle triangle, we first need to find the length of the hypotenuse. The base and height of the triangle are given as 4 inches and 3 inches, respectively. In a right-angle triangle, the hypotenuse \\( c \\) can be found using the Pythagorean theorem:\n\n\\[\nc = \\sqrt{a^2 + b^2}\n\\]\n\nwhere \\( a \\) and \\( b \\) are the base and height, respectively. Substituting the given values:\n\n\\[\nc = \\sqrt{4^2 + 3^2} = \\sqrt{16 + 9} = \\sqrt{25} = 5 \\text{ inches}\n\\]\n\nNow that we know the hypotenuse is 5 inches, we can calculate the perimeter of the triangle. The perimeter \\( P \\) is the sum of the lengths of all three sides:\n\n\\[\nP = \\text{base} + \\text{height} + \\text{hypotenuse} = 4 + 3 + 5 = 12 \\text{ inches}\n\\]\n\nThus, the length of the perimeter of the triangle that Elliot drew is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{12}\n\\]\n\nTano is twice as old as Huto was 5 years ago. If Huto will be 35 in 3 years, calculate Tano's age now.\n\nTo solve the problem, we need to determine Tano's current age given the information about Huto's age.\n\nFirst, let's find Huto's current age. We know that in 3 years, Huto will be 35 years old. Therefore, we can calculate Huto's current age as follows:\n\\[\n\\text{Huto's current age} = 35 - 3 = 32\n\\]\n\nNext, we need to find out how old Huto was 5 years ago. We subtract 5 from Huto's current age:\n\\[\n\\text{Huto's age 5 years ago} = 32 - 5 = 27\n\\]\n\nAccording to the problem, Tano is currently twice as old as Huto was 5 years ago. Therefore, we calculate Tano's current age as follows:\n\\[\n\\text{Tano's current age} = 2 \\times 27 = 54\n\\]\n\nThus, Tano's current age is \\(\\boxed{54}\\).",
